{
  "dom_id": "question-166148",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Given that $f(2.1)=21,$ approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2.1$ using a backward difference approximation.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(2.1)\\,≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=2.1.$ \n\nWe choose the step size $h>0$ to be as small as possible. In this case, $h=0.1$ since the nearest backward point, for which we know the value of $f(x),$ is $f(2)=18$ (from the table above).\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2.1) & ≈\\frac{f(2.1)-f(2.1-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =10(f(2.1)-f(2)) \\\\  & =10(21-18) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
